# F-ENVELOPE — Fixture Corpus Design

**Layout:**

```
tests/sakg/fixtures/envelope/
├── manifest.json            ← expectations. NOT inside valid/ or invalid/.
├── valid/    V01..V16.json  (16 files — acceptance floor is 12)
└── invalid/  I01..I23.json  (23 files — acceptance floor is 8)
```

## 1. Why the manifest sits outside the fixture directories

Two rejected alternatives, both of which break an acceptance command:

- **Expectation keys inside the fixture** (`"x-expect": {...}`) — the envelope schema carries
  `unevaluatedProperties: false`, so an `x-expect` key makes every *valid* fixture invalid, and makes
  every *invalid* fixture fail for the wrong reason. Self-defeating.
- **Sibling `<name>.expect.json` files** — `ls tests/sakg/fixtures/envelope/invalid/*.json | wc -l`
  (acceptance command 4) counts `*.json`, so sibling files would double the count and the number
  would stop meaning "fixtures". The valid/invalid loaders would also try to validate them as
  envelopes.

`manifest.json` one level above both directories is invisible to both globs and to both loaders.
**Do not move it.** (`design.md` §9, risk row 4.)

## 2. Manifest format

```jsonc
{
  "schema_version": "1.0.0",
  "valid": {
    "V01-proposed-minimal.json": {
      "purpose": "smallest legal envelope; PROPOSED is the only status with no promotion record",
      "status": "PROPOSED",
      "transition": null,
      "covers": ["INV-PROMO-3-negative", "INV-EV-1-negative"]
    }
  },
  "invalid": {
    "I05-promotion-basis-frequency.json": {
      "purpose": "promotion justified by how often the claim was observed",
      "invariant": "INV-PROMO-1",
      "pointer": "/promotion/basis",
      "keyword": "enum",
      "note": "the honest spelling of the frequency violation; I06 is the dishonest one"
    }
  }
}
```

The test asserts on `(pointer, keyword, invariant)` — see `design.md` §6.3 for why rejection alone
is not enough.

