## 3. Invalid corpus (23)

Grouped by the goal's three named invariants, then structural.

### 3.1 Authored confidence (5)

| Fixture | Mutation from a valid base | Pointer | Keyword | Invariant |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `I01-confidence-authored-scalar` | `"confidence": 0.9` | `/confidence` | `type` | `INV-CONF-1` |
| `I02-confidence-method-authored` | `method: "authored"` | `/confidence/method` | `enum` | `INV-CONF-2` |
| `I03-confidence-inputs-empty` | `inputs: []`, method left legal | `/confidence/inputs` | `minItems` | `INV-CONF-3` |
| `I04-confidence-value-out-of-range` | `value: 1.4` | `/confidence/value` | `maximum` | `INV-CONF-5` |
| `I21-confidence-computed-by-human` | `computed_by: "kieran"` | `/confidence/computed_by` | `pattern` | `INV-CONF-4` |

`I03` is the important one: it is a *syntactically well-formed derivation* that derives from nothing.
A corpus that only contained `I01` and `I02` would leave the invariant defeatable by anyone who
learned the required shape without understanding it.

### 3.2 Frequency-based promotion (4)

| Fixture | Mutation | Pointer | Keyword | Invariant |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `I05-promotion-basis-frequency` | `basis: "frequency"` | `/promotion/basis` | `enum` | `INV-PROMO-1` |
| `I06-promotion-observation-count` | legal basis + `observation_count: 47` | `/promotion` | `unevaluatedProperties` | `INV-PROMO-4` |
| `I07-promotion-no-evidence` | `evidence_refs: []` | `/promotion/evidence_refs` | `minItems` | `INV-PROMO-2` |
| `I17-basis-transition-mismatch` | `ACTIVE→SUPERSEDED` with `basis: "deterministic_evidence"` | `/promotion/basis` | `enum` | `INV-FSM-2` |

`I05` and `I06` are the honest and dishonest spellings of the same violation, and they trip
*different keywords on different pointers*. That is the observable consequence of the redundant
defences in `design.md` §5.3: if a future edit disarms one, exactly one of these two fixtures starts
passing, and the corpus tells you which defence broke.

`I06` also trips `minItems` if authored with an empty `evidence_refs`. Author it with a **non-empty**
`evidence_refs` so it isolates `INV-PROMO-4` — a fixture that trips two rules cannot tell you which
one is still working.

### 3.3 Deleted node (6)

| Fixture | Mutation | Pointer | Keyword | Invariant |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `I08-status-deleted` | `status: "DELETED"` | `/status` | `enum` | `INV-DEL-1` |
| `I09-deleted-flag` | valid ACTIVE node + `"deleted": true` | `""` | `unevaluatedProperties` | `INV-DEL-4` |
| `I10-retracted-without-tombstone` | `status: RETRACTED`, no `retraction` | `""` | `required` | `INV-DEL-3` |
| `I11-transition-retracted-to-active` | `promotion.from: RETRACTED, to: ACTIVE` | `/promotion` | `anyOf` | `INV-FSM-1` |
| `I12-superseded-without-successor` | `status: SUPERSEDED`, no `superseded_by` | `""` | `required` | `INV-DEL-2` |
| `I13-active-carrying-retraction` | `status: ACTIVE` + a `retraction` block | `""` | `not` | `INV-DEL-3` |

Six angles on one idea, because "no node ever leaves the graph" is the invariant with the most ways
to be violated: by naming the state (`I08`), by flagging it (`I09`), by erasing without a record
(`I10`), by undoing a tombstone (`I11`), by orphaning a replacement (`I12`), and by half-deleting
— `ACTIVE` to one reader, retracted to another (`I13`).

`I11` requires the normalised `anyOf` error mapping from `design.md` §4.4. If the mapper is not
implemented, this fixture's pointer will be a deep `/promotion/from`-ish path from whichever branch
the validator reported last, and the assertion will fail — correctly, because the interface promised
a stable pointer.

### 3.4 Lifecycle, evidence, and trust-zone invariants (5)

| Fixture | Mutation | Pointer | Keyword | Invariant |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `I18-active-without-promotion` | `status: ACTIVE`, `promotion` absent | `""` | `required` | `INV-PROMO-3` |
| `I19-active-empty-evidence-refs` | `status: ACTIVE`, top-level `evidence_refs: []` | `/evidence_refs` | `minItems` | `INV-EV-1` |
| `I20-z0-non-founder` | `trust_zone: "Z0"`, `actor_role: "builder"` | `/provenance/actor_role` | `enum` | `INV-TZ-1` |
| `I22-quarantined-without-block` | `status: QUARANTINED`, no `quarantine` | `""` | `required` | `INV-QUAR-1` |
| `I23-deprecated-without-block` | `status: DEPRECATED`, no `deprecation` | `""` | `required` | `INV-DEP-1` |

`I20` encodes `constitution/README.md:3` — the sealed layer's "only legal writer is
`kernel/amendment.py`", i.e. a builder-authored Z0 node is structurally illegitimate.

### 3.5 Structural (3, no `INV-*` id)

| Fixture | Mutation | Pointer | Keyword |
|---|---|---|---|
| `I14-missing-content-hash` | delete `content_hash` | `""` | `required` |
| `I15-content-hash-malformed` | `"content_hash": "deadbeef"` (no prefix, wrong length) | `/content_hash` | `pattern` |
| `I16-trust-zone-unknown` | `trust_zone: "Z9"` | `/trust_zone` | `enum` |

Their manifest entries set `"invariant": null`. The coverage test
(`test_every_invariant_has_invalid_fixture`) walks INV ids → fixtures, not the reverse, so
`null`-invariant fixtures are permitted; they exist to prove the plumbing (required/pattern/enum)
works at all, which is what makes a *passing* invariant fixture meaningful.

