## 4. Valid corpus (16)

Every status value appears (column 2). Transition coverage is 14 of 19 legal edges.

| Fixture | Status | Transition | Notably exercises |
|---|---|---|---|
| `V01-proposed-minimal` | `PROPOSED` | — | required fields only; `promotion` absent (legal); `evidence_refs: []` (legal only here) |
| `V02-proposed-full` | `PROPOSED` | — | every *status-compatible* optional field: `derived_from`, `labels`, `source.type: observation`, `confidence.method: decay_adjusted`. **Not** `promotion.note` — see note below |
| `V03-candidate-from-proposed` | `CANDIDATE` | `PROPOSED→CANDIDATE` | `basis: deterministic_evidence`; first non-empty `evidence_refs` |
| `V04-active-court-ratified` | `ACTIVE` | `CANDIDATE→ACTIVE` | `basis: court_ratification`; `confidence.method: court_ratified` |
| `V05-active-deterministic` | `ACTIVE` | `CANDIDATE→ACTIVE` | `basis: deterministic_evidence`; `confidence.method: evidence_weighted`; 3 evidence refs |
| `V06-deprecated` | `DEPRECATED` | `ACTIVE→DEPRECATED` | `deprecation` block incl. `prefer_instead` |
| `V07-superseded-from-active` | `SUPERSEDED` | `ACTIVE→SUPERSEDED` | `superseded_by`; `basis: supersession` |
| `V08-superseded-from-deprecated` | `SUPERSEDED` | `DEPRECATED→SUPERSEDED` | second inbound edge to a near-terminal state |
| `V09-retracted-from-active` | `RETRACTED` | `ACTIVE→RETRACTED` | `retraction` block; `basis: falsification` |
| `V10-retracted-from-quarantined` | `RETRACTED` | `QUARANTINED→RETRACTED` | the quarantine-confirms-bad path; `retraction.successor` |
| `V11-quarantined` | `QUARANTINED` | `ACTIVE→QUARANTINED` | `quarantine` block incl. `canary_id: "CANARY-0001"`; `basis: integrity_alarm` |
| `V12-quarantine-cleared` | `ACTIVE` | `QUARANTINED→ACTIVE` | `basis: adversary_cleared` — the reversibility property (`design.md` §4.3 P3) |
| `V13-demotion` | `PROPOSED` | `CANDIDATE→PROPOSED` | `basis: evidence_withdrawn`; the only downward non-terminal edge; **a `PROPOSED` node that does have a `promotion`**; carries `promotion.note` |
| `V14-reinstated` | `ACTIVE` | `DEPRECATED→ACTIVE` | `basis: court_ratification` on a recovery edge |
| `V15-z0-constitution-node` | `ACTIVE` | `CANDIDATE→ACTIVE` | `trust_zone: Z0` + `actor_role: founder` (`INV-TZ-1` positive); `kind: constitution_clause`; `basis: founder_ratification` |
| `V16-derived-multi-ancestor` | `ACTIVE` | `CANDIDATE→ACTIVE` | `derived_from` × 3; `confidence.method: ancestor_min` |

**`V13` is the fixture that keeps `INV-PROMO-3` honest.** The invariant is "promotion required iff
status != PROPOSED" — but a *demoted* node is `PROPOSED` **and** has a promotion record. If the
clause were written as a strict iff with an `else: {not: {required: ["promotion"]}}`, `V13` would be
rejected. It is therefore written one-directional:

```jsonc
{ "if":   { "properties": { "status": { "not": { "const": "PROPOSED" } } }, "required": ["status"] },
  "then": { "required": ["promotion"] } }
```

No `else`. A `PROPOSED` node *may* carry a promotion (it was demoted) but need not (it is new).
This asymmetry is real and easy to get wrong; `V01` and `V13` together pin both directions.

**This is also why no single fixture is "the maximal envelope".** The four lifecycle-detail blocks
are mutually exclusive (`envelope-fields.md` §2.1), so `superseded_by`, `retraction`, `quarantine`,
and `deprecation` can never co-occur, and a genuinely `PROPOSED` node has no promotion to annotate.
Full-field coverage is therefore a property of the corpus *union*, not of any one file — which is
exactly how `test_every_field_appears_in_valid_corpus` computes it (`design.md` §6.4). An
implementer who tries to build one all-fields fixture will produce something the schema correctly
rejects.

### 4.1 Uncovered transitions (5 of 19) — a recorded decision, not an omission

`PROPOSED→QUARANTINED`, `PROPOSED→RETRACTED`, `CANDIDATE→QUARANTINED`, `CANDIDATE→RETRACTED`,
`DEPRECATED→QUARANTINED`.

All five are structurally identical to covered edges (`ACTIVE→QUARANTINED` in `V11`,
`ACTIVE→RETRACTED` in `V09`) — same basis, same required detail block, differing only in the `from`
const. Covering them would add five near-duplicate files and no discriminating power. The floor
assertion in `test_every_legal_transition_has_valid_fixture` is `>= 12` of 19, with the five
safety-critical edges named explicitly in the test so they cannot silently drop out
(`design.md` §6.4).

`SUPERSEDED→RETRACTED` is *not* in the uncovered list — it is a distinct safety path (retracting an
already-replaced node) and should be added as `V17` if the corpus grows.

